'''New Lynn''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one member to the New Zealand [[New Zealand Parliament|House of Representatives]]. The current MP for New Lynn is [[Paulo Garcia (New Zealand politician)|Paulo Garcia]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]. He has held the seat since 2023.

The electorate will be abolished ahead of the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
[[File:New Lynn electorate, 2014.svg|thumb|right|New Lynn electorate boundaries used in the {{NZ election link year|2014}} and {{NZ election link|2017}}s]]
New Lynn is based in [[West Auckland, New Zealand|West Auckland]], straddling the borders of the former [[Auckland City]] and areas of [[Waitakere City]]. It contains the areas of [[New Lynn]], [[Titirangi]] and [[Waitākere Ranges]] to cover all the beach-side towns on the north coast of the [[Manukau Harbour]] and then up the West Coast till Bethell's Beach.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227 |title=Map of electorates for the 2020 and 2023 general elections |date=17 April 2020 |access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref>

==History==
New Lynn was first formed for the {{NZ election link|1963}}.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=268}} It has always been held by members of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. The electorate's first representative was [[Rex Mason]], who had been an MP since {{By-election link year|Eden|1926}} and who retired at the end of the term.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=218}} Mason was succeeded by [[Jonathan Hunt (New Zealand politician)|Jonathan Hunt]], who held the electorate for the next 30 years until he contested the {{NZ electorate link|Tamaki}} electorate in the {{NZ election link|1996}} instead.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/6.1%20Tamaki%20%2047.pdf |title=Electorate Candidate and Party Votes Recorded at Each Polling Place – Tamaki, 1996 |access-date=25 July 2013}}</ref> [[Phil Goff]] became the representative in New Lynn in 1996.<ref name="New Lynn 1996" />

The electorate was abolished in 1999 and Goff successfully stood in {{NZ electorate link|Mount Roskill}}. Titirangi replaced New Lynn in 1999 when population changes in Auckland lead to the creation of Mount Roskill, and Titirangi was won by [[David Cunliffe]].<ref name="Cunliffe parl bio">{{cite web |title=Hon David Cunliffe |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/50MP30551/cunliffe-david |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=25 July 2013}}</ref> Three years later, population growth in north Auckland led to the creation of the [[Helensville (New Zealand electorate)|Helensville electorate]]. The flow-on effect of this was to pull Titirangi eastwards, resulting in the reclamation of its former name for the {{NZ election link|2002}}. Cunliffe represented the New Lynn electorate until 2017.<ref name="Cunliffe parl bio" />

In November 2016, Labour leader [[Andrew Little (New Zealand politician)|Andrew Little]] announced that Cunliffe would not seek re-election at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/85954239/former-labour-leader-david-cunliffe-to-stand-down-at-2017-election|access-date=1 November 2016|title=Former Labour leader David Cunliffe to stand down at 2017 election|date=1 November 2016|publisher=Stuff.co.nz}}</ref> and the seat was won in the election by [[Deborah Russell]], retaining it for the Labour Party. She was defeated by National candidate Paulo Garcia in the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].

The 2025 boundary review saw the reconfiguration of electorates in West Auckland resulting in the disestablishment of [[Te Atatū (electorate)|Te Atatū]], [[Kelston (New Zealand electorate)|Kelston]] and New Lynn. Most of the population of New Lynn has been redistributed to the new electorate of [[Waitakere (electorate)|Waitakere]], with some communities being redistributed into {{NZ electorate link|Glendene}} or {{NZ electorate link|Mt Roskill}}.<ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{cite book |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf|title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025|date=8 August 2025|publisher=Representation Commission|isbn=978-0-473-75233-0|page=9|access-date=18 September 2025}}</ref>

